Falsifies `system_tests::user::shell_navigation_tests::two_shells_with_different_roots_cannot_name_each_others_files`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the caretaker serving the filesystem server's own root instead of the narrowed
handle it was just given. `fs_subtree_caretaker` opens the subtree it was named, receives back
a handle `r0` that reaches that subtree and nothing above it, and then serves `r0` as its
client's slot-0 "root of my namespace". Serving `fs::ROOT` instead gives the shell a namespace
rooted at the whole image, which is precisely the authority §50 says it must not have.

Expected to fail, and it does, at `system_tests/src/user/shell_navigation_tests.rs:99` on 2026-09-16:
`assert_eq!(got & want, want, "the shell rooted at {what} could not do what its capability
allows ({:#x} missing), so every refusal it reported proves nothing")`, inside the
`assert_report` helper, with `0x100690` of the expected bits missing.

**That is the SECOND of `assert_report`'s two directions, not the first, and the difference is
the whole of what this record honestly proves.** The helper checks both: a bit set that should
not be (a shell reaching past its root) and a bit clear that should be set (a shell that could
not navigate at all, whose refusals therefore prove nothing). Under this patch the first stays
clean. Nothing crossed. What happened instead is that the shell's own script no longer resolves
against a root one level up, so it reaches none of its own files and the vacuity guard fires.

**So this patch proves the test is wired to the real root handle, and it does not demonstrate a
crossing.** Saying that plainly is the point of the record: a falsification that turned the test
red is not automatically a falsification of the sentence the test is named for.

**And no patch tried in milestone 305 made the crossing assertion run at all**, which is the
finding this record carries up to notes/confinement-claims.md. The test states its property
twice: once as the per-shell bitmap equalities in `assert_report`, and once as the crossing,
`assert_eq!((a & nb::REACHED_SECRET, b & nb::REACHED_INNER), (0, 0), "a shell named a file in
the other shell's root")`. The crossing is the sentence the milestone makes and the one a reader
would quote. It sits **below** both per-shell checks, and any defect that causes a crossing sets
a forbidden bit, which `assert_report`'s first direction catches one call earlier. The crossing
is structurally unreachable: it is documentation, and the bitmap equalities are the mechanism.
That is the same shape milestone 202 found in §31's headline assertion, in a different
subsystem, and it is the second instance on record.

**The cost here is nothing, unlike §31's.** `assert_report`'s messages name the offending or
missing bits by name, so a reader learns as much as the crossing would have told them. §31's
instance cost a 234-second watchdog timeout that said "livelock".

Replayed on all three ISAs. The hunk is portable code, so one patch serves every leg. On 2026-10-03 (UTC), `cargo xtask test --arch riscv64 --test two_shells_with_different_roots_cannot_name_each_others_files` and the same with `--arch x86_64` both came back red at `system_tests/src/user/shell_navigation_tests.rs:99`, the assertion this record predicts, which is where aarch64 was already red.

